import{_ as i,P as n,d as o,e as a,y as r,q as s,f as d}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.1 Structural Domain (HV01)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.1 Structural Domain (HV01)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1283,"xkLessons":{"A.1":{"title":"A.1 Structural domain (HV01)","lead":"The variable “Structural Domain” first records candidate human objects, then judges bounded grouping gain through a grouping-gain test. A shared name does not imply a unified will, still less authority to impose dispositions.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3804–3887; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-01#section-a-1. See the theory-v3.0 source for the current full text; historical research drafts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","Short quotation: “The definition of a structural object puts forward only a candidate human object.” The variable “Structural Domain” first permits clarification of “which people, materials, boundaries, and window we are studying,” without requiring prior proof that this grouping has become an effective structural domain. It separates candidate naming from the support of a pre-registered grouping-gain test, preventing names, shared locations, or common problems from being substituted for an objectively unified will. [T-main；T-D0]","Route one (candidate object), when the object’s definition is complete but no eligible grouping-gain test has yet received support, permits recording the candidate object, material set, boundary disputes, and needs for supplementary evidence. Route one may pass object references carrying scope and uncertainty to the other ten human variables, so an incomplete grouping-gain test need not silence all subsequent description; downstream uses must not present the reference as a supported structural domain. [T-main]","Route two (effective structural domain) requires a pre-registered grouping-gain test to receive support within the same object, scale, time window, identity criterion, and extrapolation unit, and further requires the epistemic constraint “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals” (§4.8.4). It supports bounded grouping gain exceeding a threshold on preselected outcomes relative to a matched null model, with scope limited to the pre-registered scale, time window, identity criterion, and extrapolation unit. Domain grounds for an object’s real existence differ from predictive/explanatory grouping gain; gain cannot prove ultimate ontology, collective will, or jurisdiction. [T-main；T-G1]","Routes one and two are not rankings of personality or organizational maturity. Existing materials may support meaningful boundary observations without supporting a formal grouping gain; conversely, grouping gain for an outcome cannot imply all members’ wishes coincide or permit dispositions against excluded people. Cross-scale use must add units and populations, representativeness, scope of authorization, and low-visibility positions; local members cannot speak for everyone affected. [T-main]","Appeals focus on boundaries, representativeness, and identity criteria, and must be safely available without retaliation to members and affected positions, leading to review outside the original naming/decision chain. Authorized restoration does more than change the structural domain’s name: it must cancel its effect on downstream object scope, restore the material set, leave a record of the changes, and verify completion. Actual record and rights adjustments still require valid authority. [T-main]","DWP’s FRS 2023–2024 distinguishes household, family/benefit unit, and individual levels within the same survey. This provides a real-world comparison for recording object criteria, but official definitions alone cannot establish that a grouping-gain test is met, nor can a statistical unit define all family rights. The following items retain the full content of the source text and separately explain their responsibilities. [P-FRS；T-main]","Item-by-item study (“Source text” below retains the item’s complete content; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Code. The source text gives the code only in parentheses at the end of the heading and uses the name in the text. Interpretation: the code is a short identifier within the code system of Appendix B; it tracks the same variable and does not indicate that a real-world object has been proved. [T-main]","02 | Qualified code. The source text does not list this item separately. Interpretation: the qualified form serves only to avoid collisions between identical names; references to this variable must still carry object and evidential status. [T-main]","03 | Name. Source heading: Structural Domain. Interpretation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for particular people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Proposition role. The source text does not list this item separately; under §2.2 it belongs to the role of “human-domain clauses.” Interpretation: this marks it as a proposition of the human domain; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Role in the system. The source text lists it as one of the eleven human variables of Part 7 (§7.3). Interpretation: this is the definition of a human variable, specifying how it enters analysis; listing the items does not establish that any empirical test holds. [T-main]","06 | Proposition. Source text: The definition of a structural object puts forward only a candidate human object. A limited effective structural domain is determined, within the scope of a pre-registered grouping-gain test, only if that test shows that the candidate grouping achieves an out-of-sample or extrapolation gain above the threshold on the preselected outcome relative to a matched null model. Interpretation: there are two levels of eligibility: the definition of a structural object suffices to put forward a candidate; only extrapolation gain over a matched null model in a pre-registered grouping-gain test supports a bounded effective structural domain. These cannot be reversed. [T-main]","07 | Scope of application. Source text: Relationships, teams, organizations, institutions, and public issues. Interpretation: scope lists task domains that may be studied, not empirical support for every object within them. [T-main]","08 | Conditions for suspension. Source text: The object, boundary, scale, time window, or basis for treating it as the same object is insufficient for the selected route. When formal support for a grouping gain is sought, the corresponding null model or pre-registration is incomplete. Interpretation: pausing concerns dependent judgment or adoption; independent descriptions may remain, but failed premises cannot continue to be used. [T-main]","09 | Inferential premises. Source text: Structural object (§3.1). Interpretation: inferential premises provide upstream content needed for the proposition to hold; upstream candidate or support status must pass through unchanged, without automatic upgrading by citation. [T-main]","10 | Method requirements. Source text: Constraint on Object Declaration (§4.8.1); conditions for evidence (§2.5); conditions for sources (§2.7). Interpretation: method requirements constrain evidence gathering and expression; meeting method requirements does not independently prove an empirical proposition true. [T-main]","11 | Domain restriction. Source text: None. Interpretation: writing “none” means only that no such domain restriction is listed, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a listed restriction also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Objects of application. Source text: None. Interpretation: objects of application cannot replace the current object’s definition; writing “none” is an explicit statement, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Support routes. Source text: Route one, candidate object. Conclusion level: Candidate description. Conditions: The object’s definition is complete, but there is not yet an eligible grouping-gain test that has received support. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Record it as a candidate human object, and record the set of materials, boundary disputes, and the need for a further grouping-gain test. Ceiling: Candidate object description only. A reference to the object, with its boundary and uncertainty, may be supplied to the other ten variables, but the reference does not count as an effective structural domain with a supported grouping gain. Route two, effective structural domain. Conclusion level: Descriptive classification. Conditions: Within the same object, scale, time window, identity criterion, and extrapolation unit, a pre-registered grouping-gain test receives support. Further inferential premises: This grouping-gain test. Further method requirements: Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals (§4.8.4). Can conclude: Determine the limited effective structural domain within the scope of this test, and state the strength of support for the grouping gain, the robustness of that gain to tested boundary perturbations, and its applicable window. Ceiling: Limited to the pre-registered scale, time window, identity criterion, and extrapolation unit. Judgments about the object’s ultimate nature, a unified will, or authorization must not be made. Interpretation: route one may pass candidate objects to other human variables while retaining uncertainty; route two requires an eligible grouping-gain test that has received support, and further requires “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals.” Route one’s weaker conditions must not be connected to route two’s effective-structural-domain conclusion. [T-main]","14 | Can infer. Source text: Under route one, a candidate object, boundary disputes, and limited descriptive references may be recorded. The limited effective structural domain supported by the gain, and its strength of support, are determined only within the object, scale, time window, outcome, and extrapolation unit pre-registered for the grouping-gain test. Interpretation: these are permitted directions of conclusion; which is actually reached and with what strength remain limited by the selected route and materials. [T-main]","15 | Cannot infer. Source text: That naming a group makes it an objective community; that being in the same circumstances means having the same will. Interpretation: these are common boundary violations, not avoided by rewording; inspect actual inference and downstream use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale. Source text: Aggregation level covers relational or event units, the candidate member population, distributions inside and outside the boundary, and grouping rules. Spatial extent covers common locations, organizational boundaries, digital platforms, and external environments across domains. Time span covers the identification window, cycles of membership change, and boundary history. Organizational level covers roles, teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. Causal level covers originating events, interaction mechanisms, meso-level relational structures, and institutional and systemic conditions. Observation resolution covers original interactions, event sequences, individual member cases, boundary distributions, metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. Scope of influence covers direct members, people excluded, people indirectly affected, second-order spillovers, and positions across domains and generations. Network topology scope covers membership relations, boundary connections, isolated points, and bridging across domains. Scope of jurisdiction and authorization states the authorization for naming the object, adopting the boundary, and subsequent action separately. For an axis that does not apply, the reason it does not apply must be stated. Interpretation: state all nine axes individually; in particular, scope of influence creates no authority on the axis of jurisdiction and authorization, and lower observation resolution requires retention of compression loss. [T-main]","17 | Effective object. Source text: A candidate object put forward by the definition of a structural object. Only after it passes a pre-registered grouping-gain test, reaching the threshold relative to a matched null model, is it determined to be a limited effective structural domain within the scope of that test. Interpretation: a candidate object is not nonexistent; “limited effective” here specifically denotes the scope supported by a grouping-gain test, not a verdict on the object’s ultimate reality. [T-main]","18 | Kept across scales. Source text: The object’s definition; participating and affected positions. Interpretation: what is kept across scales is a comparative responsibility to be maintained during transfer, not a declaration that variable values remain constant at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Must be added when moving to a larger scale. Source text: Units and population; representativeness; scope of authorization; positions with low visibility. Interpretation: moving to a larger scale is not copying and enlarging the original table. Where materials on new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, restrict conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Changes with scale. Source text: Effective membership, relationships, and what counts as the same object can change with scale. Interpretation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; reconstruct correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Objects to which it does not apply. Source text: Nonhuman systems without intent, institutions, or interfaces for responsibility. Interpretation: the inapplicability restriction prevents unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experiential concepts; it does not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Cannot be raised to. Source text: A local group directly representing all affected people. Interpretation: inference from local to whole requires separate proof; broad impact, a grand name, or formal similarity cannot supply representativeness and authorization. [T-main]","23 | States. Source text: Candidate; identifiable; boundary disputed; does not hold. Interpretation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require time windows, grounds, and records of changes. [T-main]","24 | Observables. Source text: Differences in relationship density and constraints inside and outside the boundary; records of members entering, exiting, or being excluded; repeated co-occurrence of common problems, resource channels, or institutional rules; whether the grouping gain remains stable after the grouping rule changes. Interpretation: observable indications tell us where to seek materials; a single indication’s presence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements. Source text: The candidate object’s definition and the records used to determine whether it remains the same object; the pre-registration of the grouping-gain test and a matched null model; training results and out-of-sample or extrapolation results; a comparison of gains for the candidate and competing groupings. Interpretation: this item includes materials needed when applying for grouping-gain support; it must not be read as requiring a complete grouping-gain test before route one’s everyday description or ordinary services. Evidence gathering follows the particular route and task relevance. [T-main]","26 | Inputs. Source text: The definition of a structural object supplies only the elements of the candidate object and is no evidence that the structure holds; the pre-registered grouping-gain test with its matched null model, threshold, model class, and sample or extrapolation unit; observation positions, competing groupings, and the requirements of object declaration. Interpretation: this states what arrives from upstream and its eligibility; gaps cannot quietly disappear while passing between variables. [T-main]","27 | Outputs. Source text: Route one supplies the other variables with a reference to the candidate object, its boundary, time window, and uncertainty; route two additionally supplies the limited effective scope supported by the grouping gain. The grounds for the object’s existence and for domain facts are kept separate from the grouping gain. Interpretation: outputs identify downstream judgments that may depend on this variable; passing references guarantees neither downstream conclusions, actual repair, nor authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag. Source text: State the identification window and the lags in boundary changes and in membership changes. Interpretation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may differ in time; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extensions beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty. Source text: Record boundary disputes, absent members, and the extent of observation coverage. Interpretation: direct uncertainty toward specific materials and positions; an overall confidence score cannot conceal fundamentally uncovered positions. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone. Source text: Positions where people cannot express themselves safely, or that were not sampled, must not be represented by the aggregate. Interpretation: exclusion zones limit current inferential coverage, not delete people or facts from the world; unavailability for safety is neither consent nor absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions. Source text: Members; people excluded; cost bearers outside the boundary. Interpretation: record directly and indirectly affected parties together; visible members do not represent every cost bearer. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carriers. Source text: Relationship networks; organizational boundaries; institutional records. Interpretation: a carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something; its name, completed construction, and arrival of the required function are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible parties. Source text: The analyst who proposes the structural domain judgment; the decision maker who uses the judgment. Interpretation: identify who proposes, operates, or uses judgments; recording responsibility is not automatically a legal-liability adjudication or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status. Source text: A descriptive variable of the human domain. It confers no normative justification. Interpretation: distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status according to the detailed description. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings; normative reasons do not automatically prove empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling. Source text: A limited effective structural domain at the level of explanation may be determined only if the grouping-gain test passes, and only within the pre-registered object, scale, time window, outcome, and extrapolation unit. Otherwise the grouping gain is not determined to be supported, while the reference to the candidate object and object facts with separate grounds are retained as usual. Interpretation: the ceiling here is an explanatory-level bounded effective structural domain supported by grouping gain and bound to pre-registered outcomes and extrapolation units. Without grouping-gain support, candidate references and independent object facts still remain according to their respective grounds. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling. Source text: This variable produces only descriptions of candidate structural domains, boundary disputes, and needs for more evidence. It does not authorize inclusion, exclusion, or action. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures. Interpretation: the variable itself creates no real-world authority to impose dispositions; actual actions separately require checking choice, normative bridging, explicitly stated normative premises, valid itemized authorization, operational procedures, protections, and periods. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples. Source text: People repeatedly present in the same place lack stable relationships or common constraints; a group defined by the analyst disappears as soon as the grouping rule changes. Interpretation: counterexamples restrict unconditional extrapolation; they may require narrowing scope or gathering evidence anew, without automatically negating every bounded use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal. Source text: Under the common rule for appeal and restoration (§7.4), members and people at affected positions can raise objections to the boundary, representativeness, and the identity criterion through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection, and initiate a review independent of the original naming or decision chain. Interpretation: appeals must be safely accessible, free from retaliation, and capable of triggering independent review; a name alone does not mean authority to correct errors exists. [T-main]","39 | Restoration. Source text: Under the same rule, once restoration is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline actually revoke the determination of the structural domain, remove its effect on the scope of downstream objects, and return it to a candidate grouping. Object facts with separate grounds are not revoked along with it. Changes leave a record, and their completion is verified. Interpretation: actually cancel erroneous effects within valid authorization, correct downstream uses, and verify completion; changing document wording does not restore rights and resources to the affected person. [T-main]","Read the outputs and restoration of the whole detailed description together: route two supports only grouping gain and robustness to boundary perturbations within the pre-registered scale, time window, identity criterion, and extrapolation unit. Revoking the corresponding structural-domain determination and its downstream gain effect does not revoke object facts with other reliable grounds. Route one may still provide candidate references carrying boundaries and uncertainty; the state “does not hold” in the detailed description cannot be expanded into denial of people’s existence."],"argumentSteps":["Use the definition of a structural object to fix object, boundary, scale, time window, and identity criterion.","Without an eligible grouping-gain test that has received support, follow route one and output only candidate descriptions and supplementary-evidence needs.","When applying for route two, fix in advance outcomes, model class, matched null model, thresholds, and extrapolation units, then judge through the grouping-gain test.","Limit the test’s support to pre-registered scope; downstream recipients receive the same evidential status.","Members, excluded people, and cost bearers outside boundaries can all challenge representation and boundaries.","If a boundary judgment is withdrawn, eliminate downstream effects and verify through valid procedures; do not impose dispositions directly from the variable."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Candidate object","leftText":"The definition of a structural object has specified the study’s referent.","rightTitle":"Bounded effective structural domain","rightText":"A particular pre-registered grouping-gain test supports its bounded gain."},{"leftTitle":"Grouping is useful","leftText":"A gain holds for the specified outcome and comparison.","rightTitle":"Unified will","rightText":"Cannot be inferred from statistical grouping."},{"leftTitle":"Grounds for an object’s existence","leftText":"Domain facts establish that people or relationships exist.","rightTitle":"Grounds for grouping gain","rightText":"Compare whether grouping improves the specified outcome; neither substitutes for the other."},{"leftTitle":"Descriptive reference","leftText":"May be passed to downstream human variables while retaining candidate status.","rightTitle":"Transmission of formal support","rightText":"A reference alone cannot automatically raise route one to route two."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the source text: people repeatedly present in the same place lack stable relationships or common constraints.","Counterexample in the source text: a group defined by the analyst disappears as soon as the grouping rule changes.","Teaching counterexample: declaring that everyone agrees to the same representative because they are jointly affected by a policy skips wishes and authorization of representation.","Boundary in the original: positions unable to express themselves safely or not sampled must not be directly represented by the aggregate."],"questions":["Does absence of grouping-gain support mean no relationship or boundary may be described?","If grouping gain is sufficient, can a shared will be declared?","After the determination under route two is withdrawn, is deleting the name enough?","Does writing “none” for domain restriction mean no evidence is required?"],"diagramPath":"","caseLinks":[{"title":"Different levels of statistical objects in the FRS","href":"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Different levels of statistical objects in the FRS","text":"DWP’s 2023–2024 Family Resources Survey distinguishes three levels—co-resident statistical household, family/benefit unit, and individual—and defines the first two.","sourceLabel":"DWP：Family Resources Survey 2023—2024","sourceUrl":"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024","sourceLocator":"§2, Households, benefit units and individuals; web page lines 171–184","boundary":"Supports only the distinguishability of definitions; it does not carry out a grouping-gain test or establish any group’s shared will, general legal family boundaries, or authority to act.","href":"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.1","start_line":3861,"end_line":3944,"startLine":3861,"endLine":3944}],"outputId":"section-a-1","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: crossing a numerical threshold is only part of judging a test","text":"Suppose an outcome score for which higher is better, a matched null model, and a 5-point threshold are fixed in advance; in the same extrapolation evaluation, the candidate grouping scores 78 and the matched null model scores 70, giving a difference of 8 points, above 5. This completes only the numerical comparison in the setup; conditions such as legitimacy, independent evidence, matching of comparisons, error, and decision rules must still be fixed in advance. 8>5 alone cannot establish that a real grouping gain has received support. If the scope is this type of team this quarter, it also cannot be extrapolated nationwide or to shared will.","assumptions":"All scores and thresholds are teaching constructions, not measured samples or an actual grouping-gain test.","boundary":"An artificial threshold crossing is not used as formal “supported” status, still less as real-world authority to include or exclude."},{"title":"Teaching construction: candidate references can still be used","text":"Constructed materials concern residents of the same building, but no grouping-gain test has been conducted. Route one may fix the candidate resident scope and absent people, then pass it to the human variable “Boundaries and Interfaces” to describe entrances, exits, and resource interfaces; “a building roster exists” must not be rewritten as everyone’s wishes, nor may ordinary safety support be refused because the grouping-gain test has not been conducted.","assumptions":"The building and people are fictional; no personal data are involved.","boundary":"An available descriptive entry point does not establish its causal effects or all domain facts."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: people repeatedly present in the same place lack stable relationships or common constraints.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: a group defined by the analyst disappears as soon as the grouping rule changes.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: declaring that everyone agrees to the same representative because they are jointly affected by a policy skips wishes and authorization of representation.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: positions unable to express themselves safely or not sampled must not be directly represented by the aggregate.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does absence of grouping-gain support mean no relationship or boundary may be described?","answer":"No; with a complete definition of the object, route one’s candidate description can be used, passing references with uncertainty to other human variables.","basis":"Route one of the variable “Structural Domain.”"},{"question":"If grouping gain is sufficient, can a shared will be declared?","answer":"No; route two supports only pre-registered outcomes and scope, not unified will or authorization.","basis":"Route two’s ceiling and “Cannot infer.”"},{"question":"After the determination under route two is withdrawn, is deleting the name enough?","answer":"No; downstream effects must be withdrawn through valid procedures, a record of changes left, and completion verified.","basis":"The “Restoration” item of the source text."},{"question":"Does writing “none” for domain restriction mean no evidence is required?","answer":"No; it means only that nothing is listed for this kind of grounds; other conditions remain effective.","basis":"Appendix introduction and the second group, “Grounds and Limits on Conclusions.”"}],"figures":[],"diagramBrief":"This page uses the current text to explain the source text's distinction between object facts and grouping gain; the original research figure remains available among the historical-material downloads.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No real pre-registered grouping-gain test or results are available; all numbers are teaching constructions, and no conclusion of “supported” is given.","Representativeness, absent positions, safe appeals, independent review, and restoration for a specific object require actual records and authority."],"taskId":"S-A-1","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.1, Chinese source lines 3568–3633","claim_support":["All 39 HV01 fields and R0/R1 conditional routes."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-intro","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§intro-17-appendix-a, Chinese source lines 3562–3567","claim_support":["Five zones, 39 fields, material statuses, empty sets, and complete-route reading."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-D0","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§3.1, Chinese source lines 425–450","claim_support":["D0 definition of candidate objects."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G1","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.3, Chinese source lines 602–609","claim_support":["Conditional gain from candidate grouping under G1."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-FRS","title":"DWP：Family Resources Survey 2023—2024","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2023–2024 survey; page updated 2026-01-15","locator":"§2, Households, benefit units and individuals; web page lines 171–184","claim_support":["The same survey distinguishes household, family/benefit unit, and individual statistical levels, defining the first two."],"limits":["The researcher specifically read the definitions. They establish statistical object criteria only, not G1 grouping gain, and do not make a benefit unit a general legal family."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"A.1　结构域（HV01）; Chinese source lines 3804–3887; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-1/source/S-A-1.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"867936731fd919cc946ad855d7484d79732ab7d0fe80d4b7682bcc53c24d54f8","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-01.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-01.md"}`),c={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-01.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,m,g){const t=n("SectionLesson");return o(),a("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Structural Domain (HV01) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.1 Structural Domain (HV01) {#section-a-1}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">The variable “Structural Domain” first records candidate human objects, then judges bounded grouping gain through a grouping-gain test. A shared name does not imply a unified will, still less authority to impose dispositions.</span></div><ol><li>Proposition and Scope of Application</li></ol><p>Proposition: The definition of a structural object puts forward only a candidate human object. A limited effective structural domain is determined, within the scope of a pre-registered grouping-gain test, only if that test shows that the candidate grouping achieves an out-of-sample or extrapolation gain above the threshold on the preselected outcome relative to a matched null model.</p><p>Scope of application: Relationships, teams, organizations, institutions, and public issues.</p><p>Conditions for suspension: The object, boundary, scale, time window, or basis for treating it as the same object is insufficient for the selected route. When formal support for a grouping gain is sought, the corresponding null model or pre-registration is incomplete.</p><ol start="2"><li>Grounds and Limits on Conclusions</li></ol><p>Inferential premises: Structural object (§3.1).</p><p>Method requirements: Constraint on Object Declaration (§4.8.1); conditions for evidence (§2.5); conditions for sources (§2.7).</p><p>Domain restriction: None.</p><p>Objects of application: None.</p><p>Support routes:</p><p>Route one, candidate object. Conclusion level: Candidate description. Conditions: The object’s definition is complete, but there is not yet an eligible grouping-gain test that has received support. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Record it as a candidate human object, and record the set of materials, boundary disputes, and the need for a further grouping-gain test. Ceiling: Candidate object description only. A reference to the object, with its boundary and uncertainty, may be supplied to the other ten variables, but the reference does not count as an effective structural domain with a supported grouping gain.</p><p>Route two, effective structural domain. Conclusion level: Descriptive classification. Conditions: Within the same object, scale, time window, identity criterion, and extrapolation unit, a pre-registered grouping-gain test receives support. Further inferential premises: This grouping-gain test. Further method requirements: Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals (§4.8.4). Can conclude: Determine the limited effective structural domain within the scope of this test, and state the strength of support for the grouping gain, the robustness of that gain to tested boundary perturbations, and its applicable window. Ceiling: Limited to the pre-registered scale, time window, identity criterion, and extrapolation unit. Judgments about the object’s ultimate nature, a unified will, or authorization must not be made.</p><p>Can infer: Under route one, a candidate object, boundary disputes, and limited descriptive references may be recorded. The limited effective structural domain supported by the gain, and its strength of support, are determined only within the object, scale, time window, outcome, and extrapolation unit pre-registered for the grouping-gain test.</p><p>Cannot infer: That naming a group makes it an objective community; that being in the same circumstances means having the same will.</p><ol start="3"><li>Scale and Object</li></ol><p>Nine-axis scale: Aggregation level covers relational or event units, the candidate member population, distributions inside and outside the boundary, and grouping rules. Spatial extent covers common locations, organizational boundaries, digital platforms, and external environments across domains. Time span covers the identification window, cycles of membership change, and boundary history. Organizational level covers roles, teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. Causal level covers originating events, interaction mechanisms, meso-level relational structures, and institutional and systemic conditions. Observation resolution covers original interactions, event sequences, individual member cases, boundary distributions, metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. Scope of influence covers direct members, people excluded, people indirectly affected, second-order spillovers, and positions across domains and generations. Network topology scope covers membership relations, boundary connections, isolated points, and bridging across domains. Scope of jurisdiction and authorization states the authorization for naming the object, adopting the boundary, and subsequent action separately. For an axis that does not apply, the reason it does not apply must be stated.</p><p>Effective object: A candidate object put forward by the definition of a structural object. Only after it passes a pre-registered grouping-gain test, reaching the threshold relative to a matched null model, is it determined to be a limited effective structural domain within the scope of that test.</p><p>Kept across scales: The object’s definition; participating and affected positions.</p><p>Must be added when moving to a larger scale: Units and population; representativeness; scope of authorization; positions with low visibility.</p><p>Changes with scale: Effective membership, relationships, and what counts as the same object can change with scale.</p><p>Objects to which it does not apply: Nonhuman systems without intent, institutions, or interfaces for responsibility.</p><p>Cannot be raised to: A local group directly representing all affected people.</p><ol start="4"><li>State, Evidence, and Relations Between Variables</li></ol><p>States: Candidate; identifiable; boundary disputed; does not hold.</p><p>Observables: Differences in relationship density and constraints inside and outside the boundary; records of members entering, exiting, or being excluded; repeated co-occurrence of common problems, resource channels, or institutional rules; whether the grouping gain remains stable after the grouping rule changes.</p><p>Evidence requirements: The candidate object’s definition and the records used to determine whether it remains the same object; the pre-registration of the grouping-gain test and a matched null model; training results and out-of-sample or extrapolation results; a comparison of gains for the candidate and competing groupings.</p><p>Inputs: The definition of a structural object supplies only the elements of the candidate object and is no evidence that the structure holds; the pre-registered grouping-gain test with its matched null model, threshold, model class, and sample or extrapolation unit; observation positions, competing groupings, and the requirements of object declaration.</p><p>Outputs: Route one supplies the other variables with a reference to the candidate object, its boundary, time window, and uncertainty; route two additionally supplies the limited effective scope supported by the grouping gain. The grounds for the object’s existence and for domain facts are kept separate from the grouping gain.</p><p>Time window and lag: State the identification window and the lags in boundary changes and in membership changes.</p><p>Uncertainty: Record boundary disputes, absent members, and the extent of observation coverage.</p><p>Local exclusion zone: Positions where people cannot express themselves safely, or that were not sampled, must not be represented by the aggregate.</p><p>Affected positions: Members; people excluded; cost bearers outside the boundary.</p><ol start="5"><li>Uptake, Responsibility, Ceilings, and Correction</li></ol><p>Uptake carriers: Relationship networks; organizational boundaries; institutional records.</p><p>Responsible parties: The analyst who proposes the structural domain judgment; the decision maker who uses the judgment.</p><p>Normative status: A descriptive variable of the human domain. It confers no normative justification.</p><p>Judgment ceiling: A limited effective structural domain at the level of explanation may be determined only if the grouping-gain test passes, and only within the pre-registered object, scale, time window, outcome, and extrapolation unit. Otherwise the grouping gain is not determined to be supported, while the reference to the candidate object and object facts with separate grounds are retained as usual.</p><p>Action ceiling: This variable produces only descriptions of candidate structural domains, boundary disputes, and needs for more evidence. It does not authorize inclusion, exclusion, or action. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures.</p><p>Counterexamples: People repeatedly present in the same place lack stable relationships or common constraints; a group defined by the analyst disappears as soon as the grouping rule changes.</p><p>Appeal: Under the common rule for appeal and restoration (§7.4), members and people at affected positions can raise objections to the boundary, representativeness, and the identity criterion through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection, and initiate a review independent of the original naming or decision chain.</p><p>Restoration: Under the same rule, once restoration is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline actually revoke the determination of the structural domain, remove its effect on the scope of downstream objects, and return it to a candidate grouping. Object facts with separate grounds are not revoked along with it. Changes leave a record, and their completion is verified.</p></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"A.1"}),e[1]||(e[1]=d("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=i(c,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
